Description: Do not scan before the module-file name buffer when %f has no suffix
 After copying the source file name for %f, modfile_name() searches backwards
 for '.' to strip the suffix with no lower bound.  If no '.' has been written
 (for example a source file named without a suffix), it reads, and then writes,
 before the start of the heap buffer.  Reproduced with AddressSanitizer:
 heap-buffer-overflow one byte before the buffer at modfile_name.c.
 .
 The scan now stops at the start of the buffer.  Output is unchanged: with no
 '.' in the buffer the whole name is kept, and a '.' earlier in the buffer (in
 the -m prefix or a directory name) is still taken for the suffix, as before.
Author: Ash Pera <ashley.pera@noaa.gov>
Bug-Debian: https://bugs.debian.org/1155614
Forwarded: https://bugs.debian.org/1155614
Last-Updated: 2026-11-28
--- a/modfile_name.c
+++ b/modfile_name.c
@@ -58,8 +58,9 @@ char *modfile_name (const char *modulename,
                     for (j = 0; filename[j]; j++)
                         modfile[mi++] = filename[j];
 
-                    /* Skip the file name extension */
-                    for (mi--; modfile[mi] != '.'; mi--);
+                    /* Skip the file name extension, if there is one */
+                    for (j = mi - 1; j >= 0 && modfile[j] != '.'; j--);
+                    if (j >= 0)  mi = j;
                     break;
                 case 'm':
                     for (j = 0; modulename[j]; j++)
